            <ArticleTitle>Regulation of Calcium Homeostasis by ER Redox: A Close-Up of the ER/Mitochondria Connection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>620-632</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-2836(17)30058-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jmb.2017.01.017</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Calcium signaling plays an important role in cell survival by influencing mitochondria-related processes such as energy production and apoptosis. The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is the main storage compartment for cell calcium (Ca2+; ~60-500μM), and the Ca2+ released by the ER has a prompt effect on the homeostasis of the juxtaposed mitochondria. Recent findings have highlighted a close connection between ER redox and Ca2+ signaling that is mediated by Ca2+-handling proteins. This paper describes the redox-regulated mediators and mechanisms that orchestrate Ca2+ signals from the ER to mitochondria.</AbstractText>
